# text = But behold they were more wise than many of the Nephites; for they took him, and bound him, and carried him before Ammon, who was a high priest over that people.
# sent_id = 24
# llm_note = 'behold'(2)=INTJ discourse on 'were'(4) (no comma after 'behold' in this sentence, but still discourse marker). Main clause: 'they were more wise than many of the Nephites' — root='were'(4) as copula (VERB, since it's the predicate head). Actually per UD, 'wise' would be xcomp/attr and 'were'=cop. Let root=wise(6), 'were'=cop. 'more wise than many': 'more'=ADV advmod on 'wise'; 'than many of the Nephites'=obl on 'wise' (comparative). Second main clause after ';': 'they took him, and bound him, and carried him...' — root='took'(15) (conj of 'wise'? No — coordinate clauses). Treat first clause as root at 'wise'(6), second at 'took'(15) as parataxis (the 'for' makes it advcl/causal). 'for' here = SCONJ (causal), 'took'(15)=advcl of 'wise'. 'bound'(19)=conj of 'took'; 'carried'(23)=conj of 'took'. 'who was a high priest over that people' = acl:relcl on 'Ammon'(26); 'was'=cop; 'priest'=attr. 'high'=amod. 'Nephites','Ammon' = PROPN.
